{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeLinearReduction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeLinearReduction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chiralityCount_collapse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chiralityCount_collapse","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L218-L231","doc":"The all-pairs chirality count collapses:\n\n    2 * [ 2*C(Z,2) - C(N,2) - Z^2 ]  =  -2*Z - N*(N-1),\n\nwritten with `2*C(n,2) = n*(n-1)` so no division appears. The `Z^2` term is the\nproton-electron pairs, the two `C(Z,2)` are `pp` and `ee`, and `C(N,2)` is `nn`.\n\nNote what survives: the `Z^2` from `pe` cancels the `2*Z^2` coming from the two\nproton/electron `C(Z,2)` terms, leaving only a LINEAR proton contribution\nagainst a QUADRATIC neutron one. That asymmetry is the whole content of the\nformula — it is why `w` grows like `N^2` and not like `A^2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.one_add_hSq_div_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.one_add_hSq_div_four","kind":"theorem","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L191-L199","doc":"The `[2]_q` form of the same determinant, for `h = q - q⁻¹`:\n`1 + h^2/4 = ((q + q⁻¹)/2)^2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.detT_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.detT_half","kind":"theorem","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L168-L189","doc":"`d = -h/2` (the `pn` crossing, `c = 1`): `det = -(1 + h^2/4)`.\n\nTHIS IS THE ONLY CROSSING OF THE SIX WITH `|det| ≠ 1`. Every other factor\ncontributes `0` to `Phi_2 = sum ln|det M_i|`, so the whole invariant collapses\nto one term per proton-neutron pair:\n\n    Phi_2 = Z * N * log (1 + h^2/4).\n\nEquivalently `1 + h^2/4 = ((q + q⁻¹)/2)^2 = ([2]_q / 2)^2` when `h = q - q⁻¹`,\nwhich is the form used in the notation register.\n\n`h` is free — nothing here fixes `h = q - q⁻¹`, so the statement is independent\nof the substrate anchor AND of the Markov convention (no `z` occurs). That\nindependence is the load-bearing part: it is why the C→D excision moves `Phi_1`\nand leaves `Phi_2` alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.detT_id","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.detT_id","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L153-L155","doc":"The identity factor `c = 0`, `d = 1` (the `ne` crossing): `det = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.detT_full","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.detT_full","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L146-L151","doc":"`d = -c*h` (the `nn` and `pe` crossings, `c = 1`): `det = -c^2`, so `-1`\nat `c = 1`. Note this is the SAME determinant as the `d = 0` case even though\nthe two have opposite chirality — `Phi_2` cannot separate them, which is\nexactly why `Phi_3` is the invariant that carries the sign information. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.detT_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.detT_zero","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L142-L144","doc":"`d = 0` (the `pp` and `ee` crossings, `c = 1`): `det = -1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.detT","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.detT","kind":"def","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L139-L140","doc":"The transfer-matrix determinant of the factor `c * sigma + d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_half","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L85-L113","doc":"`d = -h/2`: the ratio is `-1`, because numerator and denominator differ\nonly by sign — `q - h/2 = (q + q⁻¹)/2` and `-q⁻¹ - h/2 = -(q + q⁻¹)/2`.\n\nThis is the factor carrying NO chirality: it contributes `0` to `Phi_3`, which\nis why the two `(1, -h/2)` factors present in every chain drop out of the count.\n\nNeeds BOTH hypotheses, and neither is removable:\n* `(2 : K) ≠ 0` — in characteristic two `2⁻¹ = 0` and the halving identity\n  `q - h/2 = (q + q⁻¹)/2` is simply false;\n* `q ^ 2 + 1 ≠ 0` — where `q ^ 2 = -1` the denominator vanishes and the ratio\n  is undefined.\nBoth hold in the substrate reading (`q` real, `q > 1`), but the ordering is not\nwhat makes them true, so they are carried as hypotheses rather than by\nspecialising to `ℝ` — §7c. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_full","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_full","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L74-L83","doc":"`d = -h` with `h = q - q⁻¹`: the ratio is `-q⁻²`.\n\nBoth sides simplify first: `q - h = q⁻¹` and `-q⁻¹ - h = -q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.symAltRatio_zero","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L68-L72","doc":"`d = 0`: the ratio is `-q^2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hecke_linear_mul_id","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hecke_linear_mul_id","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L42-L50","doc":"The recursion is exactly the one the compute uses: folding a chain factor\n`(c, d)` onto an accumulated `(a, b)` sends it to\n`(a*c*delta + a*d + b*c, a*c + b*d)`. Starting from the identity `(0, 1)`\nreproduces the factor itself, which is the base case of that fold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hecke_linear_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hecke_linear_mul","kind":"theorem","line":27,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeLinearReduction.lean#L27-L40","doc":"Two elements linear in the same Hecke generator multiply to a linear\nelement, with the coefficients given by an explicit scalar recursion.\n\n`s` is the generator, subject only to the Hecke quadratic relation\n`s^2 = delta * s + 1` (with `delta = q - q⁻¹` in the intended reading).\nStated over a generic `CommRing`: no ordering, no square root, nothing\narchimedean is used, per the §7c base-ring convention. ","signatureChunk":0}]}